/* ================= HISTORY ================= */
const TIMELINE=[
 ['Deep past','Arabica is born','Two wild species, eugenioides and canephora, cross naturally in the highlands of today\u2019s Ethiopia and South Sudan, creating Coffea arabica. Estimates of when vary widely, from about ten thousand to hundreds of thousands of years ago.'],
 ['Legend','Kaldi and his goats','An Ethiopian goatherd notices his goats dancing after eating red cherries. It is a lovely story, but it first appears in writing in 1671, centuries after coffee drinking began.'],
 ['1400s','Yemen\u2019s Sufis brew it','The first reliable evidence of coffee as a drink comes from Sufi communities in Yemen, who used it to stay alert through night devotions. The port of Mocha becomes the coffee trade\u2019s hub.'],
 ['1500s','Coffeehouses spread','Coffee reaches Mecca, Cairo and Istanbul. The governor of Mecca bans it in 1511; the ban does not last. Istanbul\u2019s first coffeehouses open in the 1550s and become centres of conversation, chess and music.'],
 ['1616','Plants reach Europe','Dutch trader Pieter van den Broecke carries coffee plants from Mocha to Amsterdam, starting the European effort to grow it rather than buy it.'],
 ['1650s','Europe gets hooked','Coffeehouses open in Oxford and London, nicknamed "penny universities" because a penny bought a coffee and access to the conversation. Venice, Paris and Vienna follow.'],
 ['c. 1670','Baba Budan\u2019s seven seeds','Tradition says the Sufi saint Baba Budan smuggles seven seeds out of Yemen and plants them in the hills of Chikmagalur, India.'],
 ['1690s','The Dutch plant Java','Coffee is planted on Java around 1699. The Typica line begins its journey around the world.'],
 ['1714','A gift to Louis XIV','Amsterdam gives the French king a coffee tree, kept in the Jardin des Plantes in Paris. Its descendants seed much of the Caribbean and Latin America.'],
 ['1715 to 1718','Bourbon is born','The French plant Yemeni seeds on Île Bourbon (Réunion). This becomes the Bourbon line, the second great branch of the Arabica family.'],
 ['c. 1720','De Clieu\u2019s voyage','French naval officer Gabriel de Clieu carries a seedling to Martinique, reportedly sharing his water ration with it on the voyage.'],
 ['1727','Coffee reaches Brazil','Francisco de Melo Palheta brings seeds from French Guiana. Within about a century Brazil becomes the world\u2019s largest producer.'],
 ['1869','Rust hits Ceylon','Coffee leaf rust is identified in Ceylon (Sri Lanka) and wipes out its coffee industry within about two decades. The island switches to tea.'],
 ['1927','Timor Hybrid found','A natural Arabica x Robusta cross is found on Timor. It becomes the main source of rust resistance for modern varieties.'],
 ['1930s','New varieties everywhere','Scott Laboratories selects SL28 and SL34 in Kenya, Caturra spreads in Brazil, and seeds are collected near Gesha in Ethiopia.'],
 ['1970s to 1980s','"Specialty coffee" is named','Importer Erna Knutsen coins the term "specialty coffee" in 1974. The Specialty Coffee Association of America follows in 1982.'],
 ['1999','Cup of Excellence','The first Cup of Excellence competition and auction is held in Brazil, rewarding individual farms for quality.'],
 ['2004','The Geisha moment','Hacienda La Esmeralda\u2019s Geisha wins Best of Panama and sells for a then-record price, launching the era of variety-driven coffee.'],
 ['2015','Processing takes the stage','Sasa Sestic wins the World Barista Championship with a coffee processed by carbonic maceration, borrowed from winemaking. Anaerobic and experimental processing takes off.'],
 ['2020s','Genetics rewrites the labels','Genetic testing shows several "Bourbon" or "Typica" varieties, such as Pink Bourbon and Sidra, actually descend from Ethiopian landraces. Coffea stenophylla, rediscovered in Sierra Leone in 2018, is shown in 2021 to taste Arabica-like while tolerating much more heat.']
];
